\section{What every job file carries}{

Four items reach every job file, and no argument controls them.
\enumerate{
\item The output and error paths, under \code{dir}.
\item A start timestamp, before the job body.
\item Peak memory, read on exit.
\item An end timestamp and the exit code, from an \code{EXIT} trap.
}

Item 3 reads the cgroup v2 counter of the job's own cgroup. The job derives
that path at run time from \verb{/proc/self/cgroup}. The root counter
\verb{/sys/fs/cgroup/memory.peak} is not readable inside a Slurm job.

A job that cannot read its counter prints \code{batchit_peak_memory_unavailable}.
It reports no number. batchit reads no second counter. \code{VmHWM} from
\verb{/proc/self/status} measures the job's own shell. It read 4,744 kB against a
payload that held 2,000,000,000 bytes in a child R process.

The option \code{batchit.memory_peak_path} names an explicit counter and turns
the derivation off. Set it where the cluster keeps the counter somewhere
else.

The trap captures the exit status in its first statement. So the job
reports the status its body exited with, and not the status of the trap's
own work.

A job file runs under \verb{set -euo pipefail}. The body stops at its first
failing command, which is what a chain built on \code{--dependency=afterok}
needs.
