\section{The version gate and its interpreter}{

A job that names \code{require_r_package} carries a version gate before its body.
The gate MUST NOT depend on the environment that starts the job. So it runs
the interpreter under \verb{env -u R_TESTS}. It also names that interpreter by an
absolute path.

The path defaults to \code{file.path(R.home("bin"), "Rscript")}, which is the R
that writes the chain. Set the option \code{batchit.rscript_path} where the
compute node keeps R somewhere else. A bare \code{Rscript} would name whichever
one comes first on the node's \code{PATH}. A gate that cannot say which R it
asked proves nothing about a version.

\verb{R CMD check} exports \code{R_TESTS}, and every subprocess inherits it. So this
matters to a package that tests a generated job under \verb{R CMD check}. A
production Slurm job carries no \code{R_TESTS}, so the unset costs nothing there.
}

\section{What \code{submit.sh} checks before it submits}{

\code{submit.sh} runs two checks. A refusal writes \verb{batchit: REFUSED:} to
standard error, exits 1, and submits nothing.
\enumerate{
\item EVERY state \code{sinfo} reports for the node MUST be \code{idle}, \code{mixed} or
\code{allocated}. The driver names the node with \code{hostname -s}. It deletes
the trailing \code{*} that marks a node slurmctld cannot reach. It joins with
commas the several lines that a node in more than one partition emits,
then tests each one. A node drained in one partition and idle in another
reports \verb{drained,idle}, and it cannot run the job.
\item A job name in the chain MUST NOT already appear in \code{squeue}. This check
fails closed. A \code{squeue} that exits non-zero refuses the submission,
because it leaves a duplicate possible.
}

The node check reads the node the driver itself runs on. Run \code{submit.sh} on
a login node, and \code{hostname -s} names that login node.
